pen to new ideas) could affect crew cooperation, adaptability.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills. Concerns: weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. Strengths: reliability, stress resilience, decision making (weather), technical competence (computer). Concerns: memory for numbers (attention/accuracy), professionalism (nasty remarks) affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate C: makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Concerns: difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education. Strengths: decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence. Concerns: communication, teamwork, adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concerns: regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. Strengths: adaptability, attention, problem solving, responsibility. Concerns: attitude (know-it-all, hot temper) affect crew cooperation.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. Which candidate balances strengths and minimal concerns? All have concerns. Let's weigh.\n\nCandidate B: strong reliability, stress, but nasty remarks is serious for crew cooperation. Memory for numbers could affect attention.\n\nCandidate C: strong decision, calm, technical, but communication issues and egocentric, not willing to learn - big concerns for crew cooperation and adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper and know-it-all attitude - could affect crew cooperation under stress.\n\nCandidate A: strong anticipation, spatial vision, leadership, but show-off and not open to new ideas - could affect crew cooperation and adaptability.\n\nWhich is best? Possibly Candidate B because conscientiousness and stress handling are critical, and while nasty remarks are concerning, maybe can